        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#where-electric-works" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Where Electric Works</a>
          <a href="#cold-weather-runtime" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Cold-Weather Runtime</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#corded-vs-cordless" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Corded vs Cordless</a>
          <a href="#single-vs-two-stage-electric" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Single vs Two-Stage</a>
          <a href="#storage-and-care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Storage & Battery Care</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Electric snow blowers have an obvious appeal: no pull cord at five in the morning at ten degrees, no fuel gumming a carburetor over an eight-month off season, and no engine to service. What is less obvious is that cold is exactly where lithium batteries perform worst, and a machine rated for an hour may deliver considerably less on the morning you need it. Here is how to plan around that, and which machines are worth owning.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Where Electric Snow Blowers Now Work -->
        <div id="where-electric-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Where Electric Snow Blowers Now Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric snow blowers were genuinely inadequate for most driveways until fairly recently. The situation has changed, but not uniformly, and knowing where the capability now sits prevents both unnecessary gas purchases and disappointed electric ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Two-stage battery machines are now real snow blowers.</strong> High-voltage models with steel augers and separate impellers handle deep snow, wet snow, and the compacted ridge a plow leaves, and they can be fitted with skid shoes for gravel. In our testing the best of them did everything a mid-size gas two-stage does, with the runtime caveat.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Single-stage electric machines are good at what single-stage machines do.</strong> On a paved driveway with under eight inches of snow, a 56V or 80V single-stage machine clears cleanly and throws well. It has the same limits as a gas single-stage: no gravel, a shallow depth ceiling, and no answer for a plow pile.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The starting advantage is larger in winter than anywhere else.</strong> Pull-starting an engine that has sat for eight months, in freezing weather, wearing gloves, is the least pleasant interaction in outdoor power equipment. Push-button starting is a bigger practical win here than on a mower or trimmer, and it is the main reason people who switch do not go back.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The off-season maintenance advantage is also larger.</strong> A snow blower sits unused for eight months, which is more than long enough for ethanol fuel to gum a carburetor. Electric machines have nothing to stabilize, drain, or service, which eliminates the most common reason a snow blower fails to start on the morning of the first storm.
          </p>

