        <!-- SECTION 2: Cold-Weather Runtime: The Thing Nobody Warns You About -->
        <div id="cold-weather-runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cold-Weather Runtime: The Thing Nobody Warns You About
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery specifications are measured at comfortable temperatures, and snow clearing happens at the opposite end of the range. Planning around that is the single most useful thing you can do with a battery snow blower.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lithium Batteries Lose Capacity in Cold</h3>

          <p>
            Chemical reaction rates inside a lithium cell slow as temperature drops, which reduces both available capacity and the peak current the pack can deliver. In practical terms a machine rated for an hour in mild conditions may deliver noticeably less at ten or twenty degrees, and the power available for throwing heavy snow drops too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store Batteries Indoors, Install Them Cold</h3>

          <p>
            The single most effective habit is keeping batteries somewhere warm and only installing them when you are ready to start. A pack at room temperature entering cold air performs far better than one that has sat overnight in an unheated garage at fifteen degrees, and it will warm itself slightly under load once running.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wet Snow Costs Far More Than Powder</h3>

          <p>
            Runtime figures assume moderate conditions. Heavy wet snow can easily halve them, because the motor is working much harder to move a denser material. If you get coastal or lake-effect snow rather than dry powder, discount published runtime substantially.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan on Spare Batteries</h3>

          <p>
            For anything beyond a short driveway, spare packs are not optional. Swapping takes seconds, and one set charging while another works turns a runtime limit into a non-issue. Budget for them at purchase rather than discovering the need mid-storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Charge a Frozen Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Charging a lithium pack below freezing can cause permanent damage, and most quality chargers refuse to start until the pack warms. Bring packs indoors and let them reach room temperature before charging rather than plugging in a pack that has been sitting outside.
          </p>

