        <!-- SECTION 4: Corded vs Cordless -->
        <div id="corded-vs-cordless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Corded vs Cordless
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both are electric, and the cord changes the calculation more in snow than in any other application.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded Electric</h3>

          <p>
            Unlimited runtime is a genuine advantage in a category where runtime is the main constraint, and corded machines are the cheapest way to clear snow mechanically. There is no battery to degrade, no charging to plan around, and the purchase price is close to the lifetime cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Cord Is Worse in Snow</h3>

          <p>
            Extension cords stiffen substantially in cold weather, becoming awkward and reluctant to coil. Worse, the cord drags through snow, gets buried in what you have not cleared yet, and has to be repeatedly lifted out of the way. Working methodically away from the outlet helps, but managing a cord in snow with gloves on is genuinely more annoying than doing it in summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cordless</h3>

          <p>
            Freedom of movement matters a great deal when clearing a driveway, a walkway, steps, and a path to the trash cans in one session. Cordless machines go anywhere including the far end of a long driveway, and the best of them now have two-stage capability. The cost is runtime, which cold makes worse, and battery expense.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            If your entire clearing area is paved, within about 75 feet of an outlet, and takes under twenty minutes, a corded machine saves real money for modest inconvenience. If you have a proper driveway, gravel, a plow pile, or areas away from power, buy cordless and plan for spare batteries.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Single-Stage vs Two-Stage in Electric Machines -->
        <div id="single-vs-two-stage-electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Single-Stage vs Two-Stage in Electric Machines
          </h2>

          <p>
            The same distinction that governs gas snow blowers applies to electric ones, and it determines what surfaces and what depths a machine can handle regardless of how much power it has.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Single-Stage Electric</h3>

          <p>
            A rubber-edged paddle auger contacts the ground, scoops snow, and throws it out the chute in one motion. It clears to bare pavement, is light and easy to maneuver, and is considerably cheaper. It cannot be used on gravel because the paddle throws stones, and it tops out around eight or nine inches of snow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-Stage Electric</h3>

          <p>
            A steel auger gathers snow and feeds a separate impeller that throws it. Adjustable skid shoes keep the auger off the ground, which makes gravel driveways possible, and a taller intake handles deep snow and compacted plow ridges. It is heavier, larger, and considerably more expensive, and it leaves a thin layer of snow on the pavement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Deciding Questions</h3>

          <p>
            Is your driveway gravel? Then two-stage, with no exceptions, because a single-stage machine will throw stones hard enough to break windows and injure people. Does a plow leave a compacted ridge across your driveway entrance? Then two-stage, because single-stage machines ride up over it rather than digging in. Do you regularly get more than eight or nine inches at a time? Two-stage again.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical note on two-stage electric machines specifically: because they leave a thin layer of snow behind by design, that layer can pack down and glaze into ice over a season if you never scrape it. A quick pass with a shovel or a light application of a concrete-safe ice melt on the driveway apron keeps that from building up, and it is the one place two-stage machines create a small amount of extra work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Single-Stage Is the Better Buy</h3>

          <p>
            A paved driveway, moderate snowfall, and no plow ridge is genuinely the case for a single-stage machine, and choosing one is not a compromise. It is lighter, cheaper, more maneuverable, faster in practice on a driveway with obstacles, and it leaves a cleaner surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Winter Use, Storage, and Battery Care -->
        <div id="storage-and-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Winter Use, Storage, and Battery Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric snow blowers have almost no maintenance, which is much of their appeal. What little they need is concentrated in the batteries and in one habit around moisture.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep batteries warm before use.</strong> Store packs indoors and install them just before starting. A room-temperature pack delivers meaningfully more runtime and power than one that has spent the night at fifteen degrees in a garage. This is the single most effective habit for cold-weather performance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never charge a below-freezing battery.</strong> Charging lithium cells below freezing can cause permanent internal damage. Bring the pack indoors and let it reach room temperature before putting it on the charger. Most good chargers refuse until the pack warms, but not all do.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clear snow from the housing after each use.</strong> Snow left inside the auger housing melts and refreezes, and a machine with an ice-locked auger will not start the next time you need it. Run the machine briefly at the end to clear the housing, then brush out what remains before storing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store at partial charge over the off season.</strong> Eight months is a long time, and lithium packs age fastest held at full charge in heat. Store at roughly 50 to 70 percent somewhere temperate rather than fully charged in a garage that reaches 100 degrees in July.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not hose the machine down.</strong> Electric snow blowers have motors and control electronics in places gas machines do not, and forcing water into the housing is a good way to end a machine. Brush snow off and let it dry rather than rinsing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the paddle or scraper for wear.</strong> Single-stage machines wear their rubber paddles and scraper bar against the pavement, and once worn they stop clearing cleanly and throw less effectively. Both are inexpensive replaceable parts, and swapping them restores the machine to like-new performance.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Never Clear the Chute by Hand</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              This applies to electric machines exactly as it does to gas ones. A jammed auger holds stored energy that releases the moment the obstruction moves, and battery machines can restart automatically once the load clears. Remove the battery entirely, wait for all rotation to stop, and use the clearing tool supplied with the machine.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

