              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric snow blowers powerful enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                High-voltage two-stage battery machines now handle deep snow, wet snow, and compacted plow ridges comparably to mid-size gas machines. Single-stage electric machines clear paved driveways well up to about eight inches but cannot handle gravel or plow piles, exactly like their gas equivalents. The real constraint is runtime rather than power.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do battery snow blowers run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 30 to 60 minutes depending on the machine, but cold and wet snow both reduce that significantly. Lithium batteries lose capacity as temperature drops, and heavy wet snow can nearly halve runtime by making the motor work harder. Store batteries indoors and install them just before use to recover much of the cold-weather loss.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an electric snow blower on a gravel driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a two-stage electric machine with adjustable skid shoes, set so the auger rides above the stones. Single-stage machines, electric or gas, have a paddle that contacts the ground and will throw gravel at high velocity, breaking windows and causing injury. This is a hard safety rule, not a preference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a corded electric snow blower worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a short paved driveway, walkways, steps, or a deck within about 75 feet of an outlet, yes. It never runs out of power and costs a fraction of a battery machine with no battery to degrade. The cord is a bigger nuisance in snow than in other applications, since it stiffens in cold and drags through what you just cleared.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do electric snow blowers work in very cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They start reliably in any temperature, which is their biggest advantage over gas. Battery capacity does drop in severe cold, typically reducing runtime and available power noticeably below about 20 degrees. Keeping packs indoors until you are ready to start recovers much of that, and spare batteries handle the rest.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Electric Snow Blower Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric snow blower disappointment nearly always comes from mismatching the machine to the driveway or from underestimating what cold does to a battery.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Batteries in a Cold Garage Overnight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lithium pack that has been sitting at fifteen degrees delivers substantially less runtime and less power for throwing heavy snow than the same pack at room temperature. Store packs indoors and install them right before starting. This costs nothing and is the single most effective cold-weather habit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Single-Stage for a Driveway With a Plow Ridge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The compacted, partly icy ridge a municipal plow leaves across a driveway entrance is denser than fallen snow, and single-stage machines ride up over it rather than digging in. If that ridge appears after every storm, a two-stage machine is the requirement rather than an upgrade.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Published Runtime Applies to Wet Snow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Runtime figures assume moderate conditions and dry snow. Heavy wet snow makes the motor work far harder and can nearly halve the time you get. If you live where snow arrives wet rather than as powder, plan on spare batteries rather than the number printed on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting It Away With Snow Inside the Housing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Snow left in the auger housing melts, refreezes, and locks the auger solid, which means the machine will not run on the morning of the next storm. Run the machine briefly at the end of clearing to throw out what is left, then brush out the housing before storing it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

